# 4-bit Ripple Carry Adder using Verilog HDL

## 📌 Project Title

**Design and Simulation of a 4-bit Ripple Carry Adder using Verilog HDL**

## 📖 Introduction

A **Ripple Carry Adder (RCA)** is a digital combinational circuit used to add two binary numbers.

A 4-bit Ripple Carry Adder adds two 4-bit binary numbers along with an optional input carry.

It consists of **four Full Adders connected in series**.

The carry output from each Full Adder is connected to the carry input of the next Full Adder. Because the carry propagates from the least significant bit to the most significant bit, it is called a **Ripple Carry Adder**.

## 🔹 Inputs and Outputs

### Inputs

* `A[3:0]` – First 4-bit binary number
* `B[3:0]` – Second 4-bit binary number
* `Cin` – Initial carry input

### Outputs

* `Sum[3:0]` – 4-bit sum
* `Cout` – Final carry output

## 🔹 Block Diagram

```text
        A0 ─────┐
        B0 ─────┤
       Cin ─────►│ FA0 │──── Sum0
                 └──┬──┘
                    │ C1
                    ▼
        A1 ─────┐
        B1 ─────┤
                 │ FA1 │──── Sum1
                 └──┬──┘
                    │ C2
                    ▼
        A2 ─────┐
        B2 ─────┤
                 │ FA2 │──── Sum2
                 └──┬──┘
                    │ C3
                    ▼
        A3 ─────┐
        B3 ─────┤
                 │ FA3 │──── Sum3
                 └──┬──┘
                    │
                   Cout
```

## 🔹 Full Adder

Each stage of the Ripple Carry Adder uses a Full Adder.

A Full Adder has:

* 3 inputs: `A`, `B`, `Cin`
* 2 outputs: `Sum`, `Cout`

### Full Adder Equations

```text
Sum  = A ⊕ B ⊕ Cin

Cout = AB + BCin + ACin
```

## 🔹 Working Principle

The addition starts from the **Least Significant Bit (LSB)**.

1. The first Full Adder adds `A[0]`, `B[0]`, and `Cin`.
2. It produces `Sum[0]` and carry `C1`.
3. `C1` becomes the carry input of the second Full Adder.
4. The process continues through all four Full Adders.
5. The final Full Adder produces `Sum[3]` and `Cout`.

The carry therefore **ripples** from one stage to the next.

## 🔹 Verilog Design

The 4-bit Ripple Carry Adder is constructed using four Full Adder modules.

```verilog
full_adder FA0 (
    .A(A[0]),
    .B(B[0]),
    .Cin(Cin),
    .Sum(Sum[0]),
    .Cout(C1)
);

full_adder FA1 (
    .A(A[1]),
    .B(B[1]),
    .Cin(C1),
    .Sum(Sum[1]),
    .Cout(C2)
);

full_adder FA2 (
    .A(A[2]),
    .B(B[2]),
    .Cin(C2),
    .Sum(Sum[2]),
    .Cout(C3)
);

full_adder FA3 (
    .A(A[3]),
    .B(B[3]),
    .Cin(C3),
    .Sum(Sum[3]),
    .Cout(Cout)
);
```

## 🧪 Testbench

The testbench file is:

```text
ripple_carry_adder_4bit_tb.v
```

It tests different combinations of:

* 4-bit input `A`
* 4-bit input `B`
* Carry input `Cin`

The testbench displays the final carry and sum.

## 📊 Expected Simulation Output

```text
==============================================
       4-BIT RIPPLE CARRY ADDER TEST
==============================================
 A     B    Cin | Cout  Sum
----------------------------
0000  0000    0  |  0    0000
0011  0101    0  |  0    1000
0111  0001    0  |  0    1000
1010  0101    0  |  0    1111
1111  0001    0  |  1    0000
1111  1111    0  |  1    1110
1010  0011    1  |  0    1110
1111  1111    1  |  1    1111
----------------------------
Simulation completed successfully.
```

## 🛠️ Tools Used

* Verilog HDL
* Icarus Verilog
* GTKWave
* GitHub

## ▶️ How to Run the Simulation

### Step 1: Compile

Use the following command:

```bash
iverilog -o rca_sim full_adder.v ripple_carry_adder_4bit.v ripple_carry_adder_4bit_tb.v
```

### Step 2: Run

```bash
vvp rca_sim
```

The simulation results will be displayed in the terminal.

